ian Greene not  long ago and there there's sometimes  this instinct that this is really going  to change things period and Public  Health is going to be done differently  the way people greet each other is going  to change permanently I tend to go in  the direction of things sort of return  to the mean and people go back to normal  and and it's very hard to get really  long lasting change for humans where are  you on that issue I'm hopeful you know  I'm cautiously optimistic but let's face  it David my colleagues and I have been  writing to our politicians and to  politicians in China for you know over  two decades basically saying look  there's going to be a pandemic if we  don't put initiatives in place that  monitor the sale of certain types of  species of animals leave you it's just  it was just a ticking time bomb it was  not a matter of if but when and you know  a lot of them would come back and say  what we caught SARS you know we took  care of MERS we've got h1n1 got a  vaccine for h1 we're do  good job and my colleagues and I were  saying yeah until we get either a long  incubation period or people who are  asymptomatic or both and then you're not  going to be ready and they didn't listen  to that so I'm very hopeful that in the  future we are able to develop worldwide  policies for other countries in terms of  how things are done and this is not to  you know necessarily be a you know  critical of other cultures for their  lifestyles and whatnot no this is really  just a world public health issue yeah  these are just facts just facts if bats  and we know bats contain a lot of  viruses and they do jump to other  species which then can jump to humans we  need to take the science and we need to  utilize that as best as we can to make  sure this doesn't happen again or if it  does we've got measures in place that  will will stop it clear very quickly and  not allow this to occur  now that's hopeful for the future in  regards to your other questions I think  early on after people start to get  vaccinated it's gonna take a little  while for people to just start so many  how to handshake or to you know  congregate in groups or whatnot it's  it's gonna be interesting is it gonna  make a world of germaphobes  I hope not I want people to be germ  aware we need to be smart about  pathogens and and and and and I'm very  hopeful that you're you're in the Boston  area where they filmed jaws you're  probably too young to remember this but  when jaws came out there was a huge  influx of students taking marine biology  right they were majoring in marine  biology when in you know Indiana Jones  came out there was a huge influx of  pious students taking archaeology I'm  hoping after this is a huge influx of  people going into microbial ecology  which is exactly what we need we need  people to understand the  interconnectedness of the very tiny  pathogens to the rest of the world it's  really an arms race between us and  pathogens and this was a virus a  relatively weak virus you know this was  I'm not gonna say easy but  it could have been much worse oh my  goodness this could have been you know  machen if this were like an Ebola right  it would be horrific it would be you  know black death time it would be not a  good time for this planet yeah the book  is so you think you can think tools for  having intelligent conversations and  getting along we've been speaking with  the book's author dr. Christopher de  Carlo  so great to have you on I appreciate  your time once again my pleasure glad to  be here
 

